<Doc
  title="MCP servers"
  description="Expose the MCP servers a project or a user configures through Exeora, so one connection reaches them all."
  canonicalPath="/docs/mcp/"
>
  <p>
    Exeora can be a client of other MCP servers and republish their tools through its own MCP
    endpoint. Configure a server on the machine that runs <code>exeora connect</code>, and its tools
    appear next to the seventeen built-in ones, under a prefixed name:
    <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    One connection then reaches the project's files, its commands, and its other MCP servers. The
    AI client never learns how to reach a downstream server directly: every call travels the same
    relay, under the same OAuth token, and lands in the same audit log.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuring servers</h2>

  <p>
    The file is the same shape at both levels, and the same shape the wider ecosystem already uses:
    a <code>mcpServers</code> object where each entry launches a local process over stdio.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Level</th><th>File</th><th>Applies to</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>User</td>
        <td><code>~/.config/exeora/mcp.json</code></td>
        <td>Every project this machine serves.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Project</td>
        <td><code>&lt;project root&gt;/.exeora/mcp.json</code></td>
        <td>That project alone.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A name defined at both levels resolves to the project's entry, because the project is the more
    specific opinion. Server names are lowercase letters, digits and dashes.
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "context7": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp"],
      "env": { "DEFAULT_MINIMUM_TOKENS": "10000" }
    },
    "postgres": {
      "command": "docker",
      "args": ["run", "-i", "--rm", "postgres-mcp"],
      "env": { "DATABASE_URL": "postgres://localhost/app" }
    }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <p>
    <strong>Only stdio servers.</strong> The server has to be a process this machine can launch.
    A remote HTTP server is not reachable this way today.
  </p>

  <p>
    Environment entries are added to the environment <code>connect</code> already runs with, so a
    server inherits <code>PATH</code> and anything else the terminal had. Values are literal
    strings; nothing is expanded.
  </p>

  <h2>How the tools appear</h2>

  <p>
    After <code>exeora connect</code> starts, it launches each configured server, performs the MCP
    handshake, and asks for its tools. On the next <code>tools/list</code> the client sees them,
    with the server's own argument schemas, descriptions and annotations, under the prefixed name.
    Two servers offering a tool with the same name is fine: the prefix keeps them apart.
  </p>

  <p>
    A configured server that fails to start is reported in <code>exeora connect</code>'s output and
    offers no tools, rather than offering tools that fail. The announcement is refreshed on every
    reconnect, so editing the file and restarting <code>connect</code> is the whole deployment.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the account URL (<code>/mcp</code>), downstream tools are offered only when the connection
    reaches exactly one project, because with several, one <code>mcp__github__create_issue</code>
    could name a different server on a different machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What a policy does to them</h2>

  <p>
    A project's policy applies to downstream tools the way it applies to Exeora's own:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Read only.</strong> A downstream tool runs only if its server marked it
      <code>readOnlyHint</code>. A tool that made no claim is treated as one that changes things,
      which is the safe reading.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Confirm before it runs.</strong> With confirmation on, every downstream tool that did
      not claim to be read only is confirmed first: in the conversation, on the terminal running
      <code>connect</code>, or in the dashboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Allow and deny lists</strong> are about commands and do not apply. Whether a server
      exists at all is a decision the person who wrote the MCP configuration made.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A local <code>exeora.toml</code> can narrow these further, as it can for the built-in tools.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What</th><th>Bound</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Servers per project</td><td>16</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tools per server</td><td>64</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One tool's input schema</td><td>16 KB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Whole announcement</td><td>100 KB</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A server past a bound is reported as an error in its announcement entry, not silently
    shortened: an agent that could see three of nine tools would have no way to know it was shown a
    subset.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>

  <p>
    A configured MCP server is a program you chose to run, with the access any local process has.
    Exeora adds the boundaries it can: the server runs with the project root as its working
    directory, its tools pass through the project's policy and confirmation, every call is audited
    under the prefixed name, and revoking the machine closes the relay path to every downstream
    server with it.
  </p>
</Doc>
